[3.036912, "o", "QUESTION how do I deploy without downtime\r\n  cache hit 1.00 · corpus cd6d0070 · depth 30s\r\nDIRECT ANSWER\r\n  Declare replicas and a Pod template; the Deployment controller keeps exactly that many Pods running and moves between template versions by creating ReplicaSets. [k8s.deployment]\r\n  Run two ReplicaSets at once: the new one grows as the old one shrinks, bounded by maxSurge and maxUnavailable, and only Pods that pass readiness join the Service. [k8s.deployment.rolling-update]\r\n  Use expand/contract: add the new column or table, deploy code that writes both and reads either, backfill, switch reads, then remove the old shape in a later deploy. [design.schema-migration]\r\nPRIMITIVES\r\n  k8s.deployment — The replica count is declared; the Deployment controller converges Pods to it via ReplicaSets. (cache)\r\n  k8s.deployment.rolling-update — Maintain enough healthy replicas while replacing the old ReplicaSet. (cache)\r\n  design.schema-migration — Every schema change must be compatible with both the old and the new version of the application code while both are running. (cache)\r\n  design.graceful-shutdown — Stop accepting, finish in-flight work (idempotent or requeueable) within a deadline, then exit -- with clients retrying in the window where the endpoint list and the process disagree. (cache)\r\n  design.health-checking — Separate liveness (should this process be restarted) from readiness (should traffic be sent here), and keep both cheap and dependency-aware in the right direction. (cache)\r\nCOMMANDS\r\n  kubectl get deployments -A\r\n  kubectl rollout status deployment/api\r\n  kubectl describe deployment/api\r\n  kubectl rollout history deployment/api\r\n  kubectl rollout undo deployment/api\r\n  kubectl get rs -o wide\r\nFAILURE MODES\r\n  selector and template labels do not match\r\n  image tag never changes, so no rollout happens\r\n  bad readiness probe\r\n  [401 tokens of 400 budget · truncated]\r\n\r\n"]
